-*- mode: org -*-

* Guile-Dmenu

This repository provides a Guile library and client program inspired by dmenu.
It includes a graphical, Emacs-shaped ~completing-read~ procedure as well as a
Wayland dmenu client.

Structured questions can now drive real graphical sessions through
~(guile-dmenu structured-prompt)~:

#+begin_src scheme
(ask-questions
 (list (make-single-question
        'mode "Choose a mode"
        (list (make-question-option 'safe "Safe" #:recommended? #t)
              (make-question-option 'fast "Fast")))))
;; => ((mode . safe)), or #f on cancellation
#+end_src

For multiple questions, each page opens through the ordinary Wayland
~completing-read~ path; Back preserves earlier answers and cancellation remains
distinct from choosing a negative option.

Call ~ask-questions/result~ when the caller must distinguish ~answered~,
~cancelled~, ~timed-out~, ~window-closed~, and ~graphical-failure~ outcomes.
The ~ask-questions~ compatibility wrapper continues to return the answer alist
or ~#f~.  ~#:auto-resolve? #t~ makes timeout use recommended choices for any
unanswered pages; it succeeds only when every such page has a recommendation.
For the common two-choice case, ~confirm/result~ uses the same outcomes and
returns an explicit ~yes~ or ~no~ answer; ~confirm~ is the compact boolean
wrapper for callers that do not need to distinguish No from termination.

Non-Scheme callers can use ~guile-dmenu-questions~.  It accepts one JSON object
on stdin and writes one outcome object on stdout; prompt contents are never
accepted through argv or environment variables.  Diagnostics go only to
stderr.  A minimal request is:

#+begin_src json
{"questions":[{"id":"publish","prompt":"Publish?","options":[
  {"id":"yes","label":"Yes"},{"id":"no","label":"No"}]}]}
#+end_src

Questions may additionally contain ~allowOther~; options may contain
~description~ and ~recommended~.  The top-level optional fields are ~timeout~
and ~autoResolve~.  A successful response has the form
~{"status":"answered","answers":[{"id":"publish","answer":"yes"}]}~.
Other termination statuses have ~answers: false~; free-form answers are encoded
as ~{"other":"text"}~.

** Feature comparison and direction

The table compares the three active product inspirations with the current
guile-dmenu implementation.  The five comparison columns follow the feature
name.  “Wishlist” is accepted roadmap work; “not planned” is an intentional
boundary rather than an implementation gap.

| Feature | bemenu | Emacs ~completing-read~ | Codex-like questions | guile-dmenu | Comment |
|-
| Vertical list and selection | yes | completion candidates | yes | supported | Core behavior |
| Horizontal menu layout | default mode | not applicable | not applicable | missing | Not planned; guile-dmenu stays vertical |
| stdin choices and stdout result | yes | no | no | supported | Preserve the lean dmenu contract |
| Fast filtering of plain string lists | yes | yes | small fixed choice sets | supported | Rich completion work must not materially slow this path |
| Selectable case sensitivity | ~--ignorecase~ | ~completion-ignore-case~ | not applicable | partial | Wishlist: expose the existing library parameter through dmenu |
| Prompt, prefix, row limit, wrap, border, height, and colors | yes | prompt and faces | prompt UI | partial | Wishlist: remaining useful vertical presentation |
| Top/bottom/center placement, monitor, margin, and width | yes | Emacs-managed | host-managed | missing | Wishlist, subject to suitable Wayland protocols |
| Scrollbar, result counter, font, cursor sizing, and border radius | yes | Emacs-managed | host-managed | missing | Wishlist |
| Pointer and touch selection | yes | Emacs input events | host UI | missing | Wishlist |
| Multiple selected menu items | yes | ~completing-read-multiple~ is separate | one answer per question | missing | Wishlist for the vertical picker |
| Password or secret input display | yes | separate password readers | sensitive context is possible | missing | Wishlist, with fail-safe secret handling |
| Text clipping, scrolling, shaping, and full Unicode display | Pango-backed | Emacs text engine | host UI | partial | Wishlist: robust display handling; Unicode input already works |
| Positional ~completing-read~ call contract | no | canonical | no | supported | Implemented |
| Lists, alists, vectors, hash tables, and predicates | string items | lists, alists, obarrays, tables | structured choices | supported | Supported for the documented Guile representations |
| Programmed completion tables | no | yes | no | supported | Try/all/exact, metadata, and boundary actions are implemented |
| Exact-match and confirmation policies | selection must be an item | ~require-match~ variants | explicit validation | supported | Implemented in text mode |
| Initial input, point, defaults, and history | filter/index options | yes | retained answers | supported | Uses explicit Guile history objects |
| Ordered completion-style pipeline | built-in matcher | yes | no | partial | Wishlist: rich engine; only pluggable substring exists now |
| Categories, metadata, sorting, annotation, grouping, and affixation | no | yes | option descriptions | partial | Wishlist: metadata queries exist, rich behavior does not |
| Completion help and candidate cycling | visible/paged menu | yes | visible choices | missing | Wishlist for the rich engine |
| Multiple completed values in one input | multi-selection | ~completing-read-multiple~ | question batch | missing | Wishlist |
| Read-only detail or explanation panel | no | prompt/minibuffer messages | yes | supported | ~#:message~ provides persistent context; Tab toggles the selected choice's ~#:option-details~ text |
| Timeout and safe cancellation | cancellation | quit minibuffer | cancellation or unanswered prompt | supported | Structured batches share one monotonic deadline and expose distinct termination outcomes; compatibility calls return ~#f~ |
| Generic yes/no confirmation API | choices can model it | separate Emacs prompt APIs | yes | supported | ~confirm/result~ preserves structured outcomes; ~confirm~ is the boolean convenience wrapper |
| Structured JSON command boundary | no | no | hook/tool transport | supported | One request on stdin and one outcome on stdout; diagnostics use stderr |
| Batch of one to three questions | no | normally separate calls | yes | supported | Opens one graphical page per question; Back retains answers |
| Choice descriptions and recommended marker | no | metadata can annotate candidates | yes | supported | Compact labels keep stable ids separate; Tab reveals the selected description; recommended or retained answers set the initial highlight |
| Optional free-form “Other” answer | filter text can be returned | arbitrary input when permitted | yes | supported | Opt-in per question; returned as a tagged answer distinct from option ids |
| Distinguish denial, rejection, cancellation, and failure | exit/result conventions | return/quit conventions | yes | partial | Structured prompt outcomes are explicit; approval-hook migration remains wishlist |

Guile readline remains a comparison reference, not a fourth compatibility
target.  Guile-dmenu already overlaps with its basic editing, completion,
history traversal, and repeat behavior, but direct readline reuse, terminal-port
integration, ~.inputrc~ compatibility, and API parity are not planned.  See
~PLAN.md~ for the detailed boundaries and roadmap order.

** Try it!

```bash
echo "1\n2\n3" | $(guix build -f .guix/modules/guile-dmenu-package.scm)/bin/dmenu
```

You'll also need

for_window [app_id="wl-dmenu"] floating enable

in your Sway configuration for the expected result (otherwise Sway will take over the window placing and sizing).

* FAQ

** What is not there yet?
- [X] Basic personalization: colors, prefix, border, wrap, item count, fixed
  height, line height (see "Configuration" below)
- [X] Supplementary Unicode keyboard input
- [ ] Complete Unicode shaping may require Pango.
- [ ] Strict compatibility with the bemenu API/configuration library is not
  planned; see the comparison table above.
- [X] Repeat
- [ ] Check some corner cases / handle all data sent as configuration by the compositor
- [ ] No pointer/mouse support (no wl_pointer listener, no click-to-select)
- [ ] No text overflow handling when drawing (long input/item text draws off-window, no ellipsis/clip/scroll)
- [ ] No window positioning (-b/-c), monitor selection (-m), margin/width-factor,
  scrollbar, or password mode: these need wlr-layer-shell/output geometry that
  the current plain xdg-shell client doesn't have access to.

** Configuration

Most of bemenu's personalization flags are supported, under the same names,
so an existing bemenu color scheme is trivially portable:

```bash
dmenu -p 'run: ' -w -l 10 -P '> ' -B 1 \
      --nb='#1e1e2e' --nf='#cdd6f4' \
      --hb='#89b4fa' --hf='#1e1e2e' \
      --fb='#181825' --ff='#f9e2af' \
      --cb='#f38ba8' \
      --tb='#a6e3a1' --tf='#1e1e2e' \
      --ab='#181825' --af='#bac2de' \
      --bdr='#585b70'
```

- ~-p, --prompt~: prompt text.
- ~-l, --list~: max number of items shown at once (default: all matches).
- ~-w, --wrap~: wrap selection at the top/bottom of the list.
- ~-P, --prefix~: text shown before the selected item.
- ~-B, --border~: border width in pixels (0 = no border, the default).
- ~--fixed-height~: keep the window height constant as the filter narrows.
- ~--line-height~: override the row height (pixels).
- ~--message~: show a read-only, wrapped detail panel above the choices.
- ~--no-input~: disable filtering and text entry (useful for confirmations).
- ~--timeout~: close without a selection after the given number of seconds.
- Colors, all ~#RRGGBB~: ~--nb/--nf~ (normal), ~--hb/--hf~ (selected item),
  ~--fb/--ff~ (filter/input line), ~--cb~ (cursor), ~--tb/--tf~ (prompt
  badge), ~--ab/--af~ (alternating row), ~--bdr~ (border).

** Differences with dmenu-like programs ?
- Powered by guile
- Not that minimal (but all Wayland clients are too)

** Difference with completing-read
- The ~(guile-dmenu menu)~ module exports ~completing-read~.  In addition to
  its prompt and collection, it accepts optional positional ~predicate~,
  ~require-match~, ~initial-input~, ~history~, ~default~, and
  ~inherit-input-method~ arguments.  Initial input may be a
  string (cursor at end) or ~(string . zero-based-position)~.  It also accepts ~#:message~,
  ~#:input-enabled?~, ~#:timeout~, ~#:max-message-lines~, and
  ~#:selection-mode~.  Selection mode defaults to ~'text~, where RET returns
  the literal input (including an empty string); ~'menu~ returns the
  highlighted row.  Collections may be string lists, string-keyed alists,
  vectors, hash tables, or programmed completion procedures.  Alist
  predicates receive the complete entry, while the key is displayed.
  Programmed tables receive ~(input predicate action)~.  In addition to
  ~#f~, ~#t~, and ~'lambda~ completion operations, the engine queries
  ~'metadata~ and ~'(boundaries . suffix)~ during pure TAB transitions.
  Metadata responses have the form ~'(metadata . alist)~; boundary responses
  have the form ~'(boundaries start . end)~.  TAB replaces only that bounded
  field, preserves text on both sides, and leaves the cursor immediately
  after the replacement.  Case-insensitive unique completion inserts the
  candidate's canonical spelling.  The compatibility call returns ~#f~ for
  cancellation, closure, timeout, and graphical failure;
  ~completing-read/result~ distinguishes those outcomes.

  In text mode, ~require-match~ ~#f~ accepts literal input.  ~#t~ and other
  truthy values accept only exact candidates.  ~'confirm~ accepts a non-match
  after RET is pressed a second time; ~'confirm-after-completion~ requires that
  second RET only when TAB immediately preceded submission.  A procedure is
  called with the input and completely controls whether it is accepted.

  Editable input supports Left, Right, Home, and End cursor movement.
  Character insertion and Backspace operate at the displayed cursor position.
  Empty text returns the first scalar/list
  default.  Pass a mutable ~(make-completion-history '("newest" "older"))~
  value to record successful nonempty text submissions newest-first, or pass
  ~(history . one-based-position)~ to start navigation at a known entry.  M-p
  walks backward through history; M-n walks forward to the initial input and
  then through list defaults.  Consecutive duplicates are suppressed and the
  ~completion-history-length~ parameter (100 by default, or ~#f~ for
  unlimited) bounds retained entries.  Passing ~#t~ disables history.
  When
  inherit-input-method is true, typed characters pass through the exported
  ~completion-input-transformer~ parameter.

  The pure completion API provides ~completion-all-completions~,
  ~completion-try-completion~, ~completion-exact-match?~,
  ~completion-metadata~, ~completion-boundaries~, and ~completion-submit~.
  The built-in
  ~'substring~ style ignores case by default; parameterize
  ~completion-ignore-case?~ to ~#f~ for case-sensitive matching.  Additional
  pure styles can be installed with ~register-completion-style!~ and resolved
  with ~lookup-completion-style~.

* Codex permission approvals

The installed ~codex-dmenu-approval~ command implements Codex's supported
~PermissionRequest~ hook contract.  It serializes prompts per user and Wayland
display, gives queueing plus interaction one 120-second deadline, and emits no
decision when the graphical prompt cannot safely answer.  In that case Codex
uses its normal terminal prompt.

Add this to =~/.codex/config.toml= yourself (the package never edits it):

```toml
[[hooks.PermissionRequest]]
matcher = "*"

[[hooks.PermissionRequest.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "codex-dmenu-approval"
timeout = 135
statusMessage = "Waiting for graphical approval"
```

Keep an interactive policy such as ~approval_policy = "on-request"~, make sure
the installed executable is on Codex's ~PATH~, then trust the hook through
~/hooks~.  ~Allow once~ sends an allow decision, ~Deny~ sends an explicit deny,
and ~Review in terminal~, Escape, window close, timeout, or a graphical error
leave stdout empty.  Override the deadline with ~--timeout SECONDS~ or
~CODEX_DMENU_TIMEOUT~.  Invalid and non-positive values use the 120-second
default.  See the [[https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks][PermissionRequest hook contract]].

** Why not use the bemenu library with guile bindings ?

That would've been an easier way to implement it. But at some point, I expected that providing a guile implementation of completing-read, it could be useful in the efforts towards guile-emacs.  For this reason, I expected it would be better to write all in Guile rather than using an FFI with bemenu (which would at some point be blocking in order to reach that result).
